.TH QD 1 "February 2026" "qqd" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
qqd \- deploy services to VMs using Podman
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qqd
[\-\-help]
.br
.B qqd
.I command
[\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBqqd\fR is a deployment CLI for multi-service projects. It reads a config file (YAML,
JSON, or HOCON) and deploys services to one or more SSH targets using Podman
with systemd or direct lifecycle management.
.PP
When an \fBexpose\fR block is configured, qqd automatically manages a reverse proxy
container on the target for HTTP routing, TCP passthrough, TLS termination,
and load balancing. By default, Traefik v3.6 is used. The proxy layer is pluggable
via the \fBProxyProvider\fR interface \(em Traefik can be replaced with Nginx, Caddy,
HAProxy, or any other reverse proxy. The proxy image is pulled and configured
automatically \(em no manual setup needed. If no \fBexpose\fR block is present,
no proxy is used.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B plan
Show deployment plan without executing. Displays per-target service breakdown
with images, build actions, deployment modes, and environment variables
(secrets are automatically redacted).
.TP
.B init
First-time setup on target(s): clone repo, ensure dirs, build/pull images, install systemd units and start services.
.TP
.B deploy
Idempotent deploy: build/pull missing images, start unstarted services, skip already-running ones.
Shows a plan and asks for confirmation before proceeding. Use \fB\-\-approve\fR to skip the prompt.
Use \fB\-\-dry-run\fR to show the plan without executing any changes.
When run without service arguments (full deploy), services removed from the config
are automatically stopped and their unit files deleted. Partial deploys
(e.g. \fBqqd deploy server\fR) leave other services untouched.
Each deploy saves a release record. If the deploy fails during the install/restart
phase, qqd automatically attempts to roll back to the previous release.
.TP
.B build
Build/pull images only without installing or restarting services.
.TP
.B update
Bump/set image version(s) and redeploy updated services. Without service args, bumps all.
Shows a plan and asks for confirmation before proceeding. Use \fB\-\-approve\fR to skip the prompt.
.TP
.B status
Show service state, image, creation time, and uptime per target.
Output format: \fBimage (YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS UTC, up Xh Ym)\fR.
Use \fB\-\-output json\fR for machine-readable JSON output.
.TP
.B logs
Stream container logs. Without service args, streams all
services. Replicated services stream all replicas. Multiple containers are
prefixed with the container name.
.TP
.B rollback
Restore the previous release on the target. Without a service name, rolls back
all services. With a service name (e.g. \fBqqd rollback server\fR), rolls back
only that service. Previous release images are pulled if not already present.
The rollback itself is saved as a new release in the history.
.PP
\fBNote:\fR \fBdeploy\fR and \fBupdate\fR automatically attempt to roll back to
the latest successful release when the install/restart phase fails. If no
previous release exists or the rollback fails, the original error is reported.
Auto-rollback is skipped on user interruption (Ctrl+C).
.TP
.B history
Show deployment release history per target. Lists recent releases with IDs,
timestamps, and service-to-image mappings. The most recent release is marked.
.TP
.B stop
Stop service units.
.TP
.B start
Start service units.
.TP
.B destroy
Stop/disable project units and remove generated unit files.
.TP
.B clean
Remove project containers and unused images from targets.
.TP
.B doctor
Run diagnostic checks on deployment targets. Checks SSH connectivity, container
runtime availability (Podman), systemd user session, unit directory,
disk space, and user lingering. Returns non-zero if any check
reports an error.
.TP
.B validate
Check config for errors without deploying. Validates service dependencies,
circular deps, port ranges, TLS completeness, health check port inference,
build strategy requirements, and mutable image tag warnings.
.TP
.B import
Convert a Docker Compose file to a qqd config file.
.TP
.B migrate
Migrate a running Compose/Swarm stack to Podman-backed qqd.
.TP
.B convert
Convert a qqd config between formats (HOCON, YAML, JSON).
.TP
.B man
Open this manual page.
.TP
.B docs
Generate command documentation (Markdown) to stdout or a file.
.TP
.B help
Show command help.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c \fIconfig\fR
Path to a config file. Can be specified multiple times; each subsequent file is
deep-merged on top of the previous ones (later values override earlier ones).
Example: \fB-c app.yaml -c secrets.yaml\fR.
.TP
.B \-t \fItarget\fR
Optional target name. If omitted, command runs on all targets.
.TP
.B \-\-rebuild
Force rebuilding images even when they already exist on the target.
By default, existing images are skipped.
.TP
.B \-\-approve
Skip the interactive confirmation prompt (for CI/scripts).
Applies to \fBdeploy\fR and \fBupdate\fR commands.
.TP
.B \-\-no-build
Skip all image builds. Applies to \fBdeploy\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-config-only
Update config and restart services without syncing or building.
Applies to \fBdeploy\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-dry-run
Show the deployment plan without executing any changes.
Applies to \fBdeploy\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-output \fIjson\fR
Machine-readable output format for \fBstatus\fR command.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show help for the command.
.TP
.B \-\-format \fImarkdown\fR
Documentation output format for \fBqqd docs\fR (currently markdown only).
.TP
.B \-o, \-\-output \fIpath\fR
Write generated docs to a file for \fBqqd docs\fR.
.SH LOCAL TARGETS
Set \fBhost = "local"\fR in a target definition to execute commands on the
local machine instead of over SSH. The \fBuser\fR and \fBssh_key\fR fields
are not required for local targets.
.SH CONFIG
\fBqqd\fR reads YAML, JSON, or HOCON config files. Format is auto-detected from the file
extension. The config defines services, targets, and an optional reverse proxy via the
per-target \fBexpose\fR block.
.SS Expose (Reverse Proxy)
The \fBexpose\fR block is defined inside each target and configures host-port listeners.
Top-level keys are host port numbers. Traefik is used as the reverse proxy.
Different targets can have different expose configurations.
.PP
\fBHTTP routing\fR \(em map value with path \(-> service:port routes:
.RS
.nf
targets {
  prod {
    host = "192.0.2.10"
    user = "ec2-user"
    repo_dir = "/home/ec2-user/app"
    expose {
      9999 {
        "/api/" = "server:8080"
        "/files/" = "files:9998"
        "/" = "frontend:80"
      }
    }
  }
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBTCP passthrough\fR \(em string value for raw TCP forwarding:
.RS
.nf
targets {
  prod {
    expose {
      5432 = "db:5432"
      8428 = "victoria:8428"
    }
  }
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBHTTP with TLS\fR \(em add a \fBtls\fR sub-key inside an HTTP entry:
.RS
.nf
targets {
  prod {
    expose {
      80 {
        "/" = "server:8080"
        tls {
          port = 443
          certs_dir = "${CERTS_DIR}"
          server_name = "example.com"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBseveral hostnames on one certificate\fR \(em pass a list; the first entry names
the certificate directory under \fIcerts_dir\fR/live/ and every entry is redirected
from HTTP to HTTPS:
.RS
.nf
tls {
  port = 443
  certs_dir = "/etc/letsencrypt"
  server_name = ["primary.example.com", "alias.example.com"]
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBTraefik dashboard\fR \(em expose Traefik's built-in dashboard on a host port:
.RS
.nf
targets {
  prod {
    expose {
      dashboard = 1111
      80 {
        "/" = "server:8080"
      }
    }
  }
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
The dashboard is accessible at \fBhttp://<host>:1111\fR and maps to Traefik's internal
API port (8080). Set \fBdashboard = 0\fR or remove the key to disable.
Adding or removing the dashboard triggers a proxy container restart on deploy.
.PP
\fBRules:\fR
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBexpose\fR is defined per-target inside each target block
.IP \(bu 2
Top-level keys = host port numbers
.IP \(bu 2
String target format: "service_name:container_port"
.IP \(bu 2
Map value = HTTP entry with path routes
.IP \(bu 2
String value = TCP passthrough
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtls\fR sub-key inside HTTP entries for HTTPS termination
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdashboard = <port>\fR enables Traefik's web UI on the given host port
.RE
.PP
When the \fBexpose\fR block is present, qqd generates:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
A static Traefik config at \fB~/.config/qqd/<project>/traefik.yml\fR
.IP \(bu 2
A dynamic routes config at \fB~/.config/qqd/<project>/dynamic/routes.yml\fR
.IP \(bu 2
A \fB<project>-proxy\fR container (traefik:v3.6) that publishes the configured ports
.RE
.PP
Dynamic route config changes are auto-reloaded by Traefik without restarting the proxy.
Entrypoint changes (adding/removing host ports) and proxy Quadlet changes
(e.g. adding/removing dashboard) trigger a proxy restart.
.SS Deploy Hooks
Project-level and per-service hooks run shell commands at deployment boundaries.
.RS
.nf
hooks {
    pre_deploy = "echo starting deploy"
    post_deploy = "./notify.sh"
    pre_build = "echo preparing build"
    post_build = "echo build complete"
}
services {
    server {
        image = "ghcr.io/acme/server:1.0"
        hooks {
            pre_build = "echo building server"
            post_build = "echo server built"
        }
    }
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Project hooks: \fBpre_deploy\fR, \fBpost_deploy\fR, \fBpre_build\fR, \fBpost_build\fR.
Service hooks: \fBpre_build\fR, \fBpost_build\fR.
Hook failure aborts the deployment with a descriptive error.
Hooks run on the appropriate executor (target for deploy/github-actions, build host for build-host strategy).
.SS Env Files
Load environment variables from files using \fBenv_file\fR at project or service level:
.RS
.nf
env_file = ".env"
env_file = [".env", ".env.prod"]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Multiple files use last-wins precedence (later files override earlier ones).
Explicit \fBenv {}\fR values always take priority over file values.
Supports \fBKEY=value\fR, quoted values, \fB# comments\fR, and \fBexport\fR prefix.
.SS Build Strategies
The \fBbuild\fR block configures where images are built:
.RS
.nf
build {
    strategy = "local"          # default: build on the target itself
}
build {
    strategy = "build-host"     # build on a dedicated build server
    host = "192.0.2.15"
    user = "builder"
    ssh_key = "~/.ssh/build-key"
}
build {
    strategy = "github-actions" # pull pre-built images (built by CI)
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Build config can be set at project level and overridden per-target.
Target build config is merged with project build config (target values override).
.SS Release History
Each deploy automatically saves a release record mapping service names to images.
Records are stored per target at \fB~/.config/qqd/<project>/releases/<id>.json\fR.
The last 10 releases are kept (older ones are trimmed automatically).
Use \fBqqd history\fR to view and \fBqqd rollback\fR to restore a previous release.
.SS Health Checks
Health checks use an object form with explicit path and port:
.RS
.nf
health { path = "/api/health", port = 8080 }
.fi
.RE
.PP
A string shorthand is also supported (port must be specified in the object form
if the health check needs a specific port):
.RS
.nf
health = "/api/health"
.fi
.RE
.PP
When configured, containers get a health check that enables rolling restarts
for replicated services.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fB~/.config/containers/systemd/\fR
Generated unit files on targets.
.TP
\fB~/.config/qqd/<project>/traefik.yml\fR
Generated Traefik static config on targets.
.TP
\fB~/.config/qqd/<project>/dynamic/routes.yml\fR
Generated Traefik dynamic routes config on targets.
.TP
\fB~/.config/qqd/<project>/releases/<id>.json\fR
Release history records on targets (one per deploy/rollback).
.TP
\fB~/.local/bin/qqd\fR
Installed executable path (via install.sh).
.TP
\fB~/.local/share/man/man1/qqd.1\fR
Installed manual page path (via install.sh).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
qqd plan -c app.yaml
Preview what deploy would do.
.TP
qqd deploy -c app.yaml
Deploy all services to all targets.
.TP
qqd deploy -c app.yaml --dry-run
Show plan without executing.
.TP
qqd deploy -c app.yaml -t main server
Deploy a single service on one target.
.TP
qqd update -c app.yaml server
Auto-bump server image tag and deploy.
.TP
qqd status -c app.yaml --output json
Machine-readable status output.
.TP
qqd rollback -c app.yaml
Restore previous release on all targets.
.TP
qqd rollback -c app.yaml server
Rollback only the server service.
.TP
qqd history -c app.yaml
Show release history.
.TP
qqd doctor -c app.yaml
Run diagnostic checks on targets.
.TP
qqd validate -c app.yaml
Check config for errors.
.TP
qqd help deploy
Show usage for deploy command.
.TP
qqd docs -o docs/CLI.md
Generate Markdown command reference into a file.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR man (1),
.BR podman (1),
.BR systemd (1)
